.TH "PING-BULK" "1" "" "" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ping-bulk \- monitor multiple hosts with continuous ping
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBping\-bulk\fP [\fB\-f\fP \fIFILE\fP] [\fB\-l\fP \fILOGFILE\fP] [\fIHOST\fP ...]
\fBping\-bulk\fP \fB\-f\fP \fIFILE\fP
.fi
.sp
.SH DESCRIPTION
.sp
\fBping\-bulk\fP monitors one or more hosts simultaneously using a
persistent \fBping\fP subprocess per host.  Results are shown in a
continuously updated terminal interface with a rolling ping\-history
strip, colour\-coded status, optional statistics columns, a scrollable
event log, and SSH\-based remote monitoring.
.sp
The program requires no third\-party Python packages.  Only the Python
3 standard library and the system \fBping\fP binary (iputils\-ping, which
supports \fB\-O\fP and \fB\-D\fP) are needed.
.SH OPTIONS
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB\-f FILE\fP, \fB\-\-file FILE\fP
Read hosts from \fIFILE\fP instead of (or in addition to) positional
arguments.  See \fBHOSTS FILE\fP below for the file format.
.TP
.B \fB\-l LOGFILE\fP, \fB\-\-log\-file LOGFILE\fP
Append every event\-log entry to \fILOGFILE\fP in real time.
Overrides any \fBlog =\fP setting in the config file.
.TP
.B \fBHOST ...\fP
One or more host names or IP addresses to ping.  Brace expansion
is supported (see \fBBRACE EXPANSION\fP below).
Tokens beginning with \fB:\fP are treated as startup commands
(same syntax as the interactive \fB:\fP command line).
.UNINDENT
.SH KEYBOARD REFERENCE
.SS Navigation
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBq\fP / \fBQ\fP
Quit.
.TP
.B \fB?\fP
Open the built\-in help overlay.  Close with \fBq\fP, \fBQ\fP, or \fBEsc\fP\&.
.TP
.B \fB:\fP
Open the command line (see \fBCOMMANDS\fP below).
.TP
.B \fB←\fP / \fB→\fP
Scroll the ping\-history strip backwards / forwards in time.
.TP
.B \fB↑\fP / \fB↓\fP
Scroll the event log up / down one line.
.TP
.B \fBPgUp\fP / \fBPgDn\fP
Scroll the event log up / down one page.
.TP
.B \fBSpace\fP
Insert a timestamped \fIseen\fP separator into the event log.
.UNINDENT
.SS Display
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBd\fP / \fBD\fP
Cycle the DNS display mode forward / backward.
Modes: \fBoff\fP → \fBhostname\fP → \fBip\fP\&.
.TP
.B \fBs\fP / \fBS\fP
Cycle the stats column forward / backward.
Modes: \fBoff\fP → \fBLoss%\fP → \fBAvg\fP → \fBMin\fP → \fBMax\fP →
\fBStDev\fP → \fBRX\fP → \fBTX\fP → \fBXX\fP → \fBAll\fP\&.
.TP
.B \fBo\fP / \fBO\fP
Cycle the sort order forward / backward.
Orders: \fBnone\fP → \fBname\fP → \fBstatus\fP → \fBlatency\fP\&.
.TP
.B \fBh\fP / \fBH\fP
Cycle the ping\-history display mode forward / backward.
Modes: \fBsuccess\fP → \fBrtt\fP → \fBscaled\fP\&.
.TP
.B \fBp\fP / \fBP\fP
Toggle pause (freeze the display without stopping pings).
.TP
.B \fBC\fP
Open the \fIclear event log\fP confirmation prompt.
.UNINDENT
.SH COMMANDS
.sp
Commands are entered at the \fB:\fP prompt.  The leading \fB:\fP is
optional when the command is used inside a hosts file.
.SS Quitting
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB:q\fP, \fB:quit\fP
Quit the application.
.UNINDENT
.SS Display
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB:dns [off|hostname|ip]\fP
With no argument, cycle the DNS display mode forward.
With an argument, set the mode directly.
.TP
.B \fB:stats [mode]\fP
With no argument, cycle the stats column forward.
With an argument, set the column directly.
Valid modes: \fBoff\fP, \fBLoss%\fP, \fBAvg\fP, \fBMin\fP, \fBMax\fP,
\fBStDev\fP, \fBRX\fP, \fBTX\fP, \fBXX\fP, \fBAll\fP\&.
.TP
.B \fB:sort [mode]\fP
With no argument, cycle the sort order forward.
With an argument, set the order directly.
Valid modes: \fBnone\fP, \fBname\fP, \fBstatus\fP, \fBlatency\fP\&.
.TP
.B \fB:history [mode]\fP
With no argument, cycle the history mode forward.
With an argument, set the mode directly.
Valid modes: \fBsuccess\fP, \fBrtt\fP, \fBscaled\fP\&.
.TP
.B \fB:pause\fP
Toggle pause on/off.
.UNINDENT
.SS Event log
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB:seen\fP
Insert a timestamped \fIseen\fP separator into the event log.
.TP
.B \fB:clear\fP
Clear the event log (with confirmation prompt).
.TP
.B \fB:log [file|off]\fP
With no argument, print the active log file path (or report that
logging is off).  With a file path, save the current log to that
file and begin streaming future events there.  With \fBoff\fP,
disable streaming.
.UNINDENT
.SS Configuration
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB:saveconfig\fP
Save current display settings (dns, stats, sort, history, log) to
the XDG config file (\fB~/.config/ping\-bulk/config\fP).
.UNINDENT
.SS Hosts and DNS
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB:resolv <ip\-pattern> <hostname\-template>\fP
Register a static name↔IP mapping.  \fIip\-pattern\fP may use brace
expansion; \fB\eN\fP in \fIhostname\-template\fP is replaced by the value
produced by brace group \fIN\fP (1\-based) for each expanded IP.
.sp
Example:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
:resolv 10.0.1.{1..4} leaf\-sw\e1
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This registers \fB10.0.1.1\(ga\(ga→\(ga\(galeaf\-sw1\fP, \fB10.0.1.2\(ga\(ga→\(ga\(galeaf\-sw2\fP,
etc., so those IPs display with meaningful names and the \fBping\fP
subprocess is also given the IP directly.
.UNINDENT
.SS SSH monitoring
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB:ssh [opts] <dest> <ping\-host>\fP
Add a monitor that runs \fBping \-O \-D <ping\-host>\fP on the remote
machine \fIdest\fP via \fBssh \-o BatchMode=yes\fP\&.  \fIopts\fP may include any
SSH flags (e.g. \fB\-J bastion\fP).  The last token is always the
ping target; everything else is the SSH command line.
.sp
Examples:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
:ssh user@remote 8.8.8.8
:ssh \-J bastion ops@remote\-a 10.10.0.1
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B \fB:ssh\-begin [opts] <dest>\fP
Open an SSH block.  Every plain host line that follows (until
\fB:ssh\-end\fP) is automatically wrapped as
\fB:ssh [opts] <dest> <host>\fP\&.  Only valid inside a hosts file.
.TP
.B \fB:ssh\-end\fP
Close the current \fB:ssh\-begin\fP block.  Only valid inside a
hosts file.
.UNINDENT
.SS Help
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB:help\fP
Open the built\-in help overlay (same as \fB?\fP).
.UNINDENT
.SH HOSTS FILE
.sp
A hosts file lists one entry per line.  Lines are processed in order.
.SS Format
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB# comment\fP
Comment line — ignored entirely.
.TP
.B (empty line)
Ignored.
.TP
.B \fB## Title\fP or \fB:title Title\fP
Insert a section\-header row in the display (display only; not
pinged).
.TP
.B \fBhostname\-or\-ip\fP
A host or IP address to monitor.  Brace expansion is applied.
.TP
.B \fB:cmd [args]\fP
Any command listed under \fBCOMMANDS\fP above; applied immediately
when the file is loaded.
.UNINDENT
.SS Shebang support
.sp
A hosts file may include a shebang on its first line so that it can be
run directly like a script:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#!/usr/bin/env \-S ping\-bulk \-f
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Make the file executable and invoke it directly:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
chmod +x myhosts
\&./myhosts
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBWhy\fP \fBenv \-S\fP \fB?\fP
The Linux kernel only passes a \fIsingle\fP argument string to the
interpreter named in a \fB#!\fP line.  Without \fB\-S\fP, \fBenv\fP would
try to find a binary literally named \fBping\-bulk \-f\fP, which does
not exist.  The \fB\-S\fP flag (\fB\-\-split\-string\fP) tells \fBenv\fP to
split that single argument on whitespace before executing, so
\fBping\-bulk\fP and \fB\-f\fP are passed as two separate tokens.
.TP
.B \fBPortability\fP
\fBenv \-S\fP requires \fBGNU coreutils ≥ 8.30\fP (Linux, available in
Debian 10+, Ubuntu 20.04+, Fedora 29+) or \fBmacOS 12 Monterey or
later\fP (which ships a compatible \fB/usr/bin/env\fP).
.sp
On older systems that lack \fBenv \-S\fP you can use a small wrapper
script instead:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#!/bin/sh
exec ping\-bulk \-f \(dq$0\(dq \(dq$@\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Place that block as the first two lines of the hosts file and
\fBchmod +x\fP it as usual.  The \fB$0\fP passes the file\(aqs own path to
\fB\-f\fP, and \fB$@\fP forwards any additional arguments.
.TP
.B \fBPassing extra flags via the shebang\fP
Additional flags supported by \fBping\-bulk\fP (such as \fB\-l\fP) can be
appended to the shebang line:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#!/usr/bin/env \-S ping\-bulk \-f \-l /var/log/myhosts.log
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Because \fB\-S\fP splits on whitespace, all tokens after \fBenv \-S\fP
are passed verbatim to \fBping\-bulk\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.SH BRACE EXPANSION
.sp
Brace expansion applies to host/IP arguments and to the IP side of
\fB:resolv\fP commands.
.sp
Supported forms:
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB{a,b,c}\fP
Comma\-separated list — expands to each item in turn.
.TP
.B \fB{n..m}\fP
Inclusive integer range — expands to every integer from \fIn\fP to \fIm\fP\&.
A descending range (\fB{4..1}\fP) is supported.
.TP
.B \fB{n\-m}\fP
Same as \fB{n..m}\fP (zsh\-style dash separator).
.UNINDENT
.sp
Mixed items (\fB{10,11..13,15}\fP) are allowed within a single group.
Multiple brace groups in a single string produce the \fBcartesian
product\fP of all groups.  Nested braces are expanded inside\-out.
.sp
Examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
10.0.0.{1,2,5}          → 10.0.0.1  10.0.0.2  10.0.0.5
10.0.0.{1..4}           → 10.0.0.1 … 10.0.0.4
10.0.0.{1\-4}            → same (zsh\-style)
10.{1..2}.0.{1,5}       → 4 addresses (cartesian product)
web{1..3}.example.com   → web1.example.com … web3.example.com
10.0.0.{10,11..12,14}   → .10 .11 .12 .14
10.0.{1..2}.{10,2{1,2}} → 8 addresses (nested braces)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH HISTORY MODES
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBsuccess\fP
One character per ping: \fB\&.\fP success, \fBX\fP timeout, \fB?\fP
process error.
.TP
.B \fBrtt\fP
Numeric RTT (ms) right\-justified in a fixed\-width cell.  Cell
width adapts to the largest RTT seen.
.TP
.B \fBscaled\fP
Digit 0–9 mapping 10 ms buckets: 0 = <10 ms, 1 = 10–19 ms, …,
9 = 90–99 ms, \fB>\fP = ≥ 100 ms.
.UNINDENT
.SH STATS COLUMNS
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBoff\fP
Show the last RTT only (colour\-coded green/yellow).
.TP
.B \fBLoss%\fP
Packet\-loss percentage since startup.
.TP
.B \fBAvg\fP
Average RTT (ms) of all successful pings since startup.
.TP
.B \fBMin\fP / \fBMax\fP
Minimum / maximum RTT since startup.
.TP
.B \fBStDev\fP
Population standard deviation of all RTT samples.
.TP
.B \fBRX\fP
Total successful pings received.
.TP
.B \fBTX\fP
Total pings sent (RX + XX).
.TP
.B \fBXX\fP
Total lost pings (timeouts).
.TP
.B \fBAll\fP
All of the above in a single wide row.
.UNINDENT
.SH DNS MODES
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBoff\fP
Display the name exactly as given on the command line.
.TP
.B \fBhostname\fP
Reverse\-DNS lookup: IP → hostname.  Forward lookup for hostnames.
Falls back to the original value on failure.
.TP
.B \fBip\fP
Forward\-DNS lookup: hostname → IP.  Pass\-through for IPs.
Falls back to the original value on failure.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Resolution runs in a background thread and does not block the UI.
Static \fB:resolv\fP mappings take priority over DNS and are applied
immediately.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE
.sp
Settings are stored in \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ping\-bulk/config\fP
(defaults to \fB~/.config/ping\-bulk/config\fP).
.sp
A skeleton file with commented defaults is created on first run.
Use \fB:saveconfig\fP to write the current settings, or edit the file
directly.
.SS Recognised keys
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBdns = off|hostname|ip\fP
DNS display mode.
.TP
.B \fBstats = off|Loss%|Avg|Min|Max|StDev|RX|TX|XX|All\fP
Stats column.
.TP
.B \fBsort = none|name|status|latency\fP
Sort order.
.TP
.B \fBhistory = success|rtt|scaled\fP
History display mode.
.TP
.B \fBlog = /path/to/file\fP
Path to stream the event log; leave blank to disable.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# ping\-bulk configuration
dns     = hostname
stats   = Loss%
sort    = status
history = scaled
log     = /var/log/ping\-bulk.log
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH EVENT LOG
.sp
\fBping\-bulk\fP records the following events:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Host starts up (first successful ping after launch).
.IP \(bu 2
Host goes down (first timeout after being up).
.IP \(bu 2
Host recovers (first successful ping after being down), including
total downtime.
.IP \(bu 2
Fatal process errors (DNS resolution failure, permission denied, …).
.UNINDENT
.sp
Each line is prefixed with an ISO 8601 timestamp including timezone
offset:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2026\-03\-20T14:05:32+0200   192.168.1.1   host down
2026\-03\-20T14:06:10+0200   192.168.1.1   host recover. Down time: 38 sec
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The log is scrollable in the UI (\fB↑\fP/\fB↓\fP/\fBPgUp\fP/\fBPgDn\fP) and
can be streamed to a file with \fB\-l\fP/\fB\-\-log\-file\fP or \fB:log\fP\&.
.SH SSH MONITORING
.sp
Remote monitoring requires passwordless SSH access (key\-based
authentication or an active ssh\-agent).  The connection uses
\fBBatchMode=yes\fP so no password prompt ever appears.
.sp
Fatal errors (permission denied, host\-key verification failure, too
many authentication failures) stop the monitor permanently.  Transient
errors (connection refused, no route to host, timeout) trigger an
automatic retry with exponential backoff (1 s → 2 s → 4 s … capped at
30 s).
.sp
The display label for an SSH monitor is \fBdest→ping\-host\fP, e.g.
\fBops@remote\-a→10.10.0.1\fP\&.
.SH EXAMPLES
.sp
Ping a few public resolvers:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
ping\-bulk 8.8.8.8 1.1.1.1 9.9.9.9
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Load hosts from a file and stream events to a log:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
ping\-bulk \-f /etc/ping\-bulk/hosts.txt \-l /var/log/ping\-bulk.log
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Use brace expansion to monitor a whole subnet:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
ping\-bulk 10.0.0.{1..254}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Combine a hosts file with extra command\-line hosts:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
ping\-bulk \-f office.hosts 8.8.8.8 1.1.1.1
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Run an executable hosts file directly:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
chmod +x office.hosts
\&./office.hosts
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Monitor remote hosts via an SSH jump host:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
:ssh \-J bastion.example.com ops@remote 10.10.0.{1..8}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH FILES
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fB~/.config/ping\-bulk/config\fP
User configuration file (XDG: \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ping\-bulk/config\fP).
.UNINDENT
.SH SEE ALSO
.sp
\fBping\fP(8), \fBssh\fP(1)
